        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"answer_text\":\"No. A PSEN1-related finding and lecanemab\u2019s Alzheimer\u2019s indication do not, by themselves, establish that lecanemab should be given because of that finding. Treatment eligibility depends on the specific diagnosis, disease stage, confirmation of amyloid pathology, and the applicable prescribing criteria and safety assessment. Whether a particular PSEN1 variant changes the expected benefit or risk is uncertain; the finding alone is not a treatment indication. This is an unverified development-control answer, not clinical advice.\",\"claims\":[],\"unanswered\":[\"Which PSEN1 variant or finding is involved, and how is it classified?\",\"Does the patient meet the applicable lecanemab eligibility and safety criteria?\",\"What evidence, if any, establishes lecanemab\u2019s benefit and risk specifically in patients with this PSEN1 finding?\"]}"
        }
      ],
      "phase": "final_answer",
      "role": "assistant"
    }
